WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
Could two different numbers share the same factors or meet in the same multiples pattern?

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:09.864
Today we are connecting factors, multiples and divisibility.

00:00:10.084 --> 00:00:15.833
We will compare sets, identify shared relationships, and explain why a pattern continues.

00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:20.910
Our learning goal is to explain shared factors and shared multiples.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.317
Success criteria.

00:00:23.537 --> 00:00:26.666
I can compare factor lists for shared values.

00:00:26.886 --> 00:00:30.544
I can compare equal-group patterns for shared multiples.

00:00:30.764 --> 00:00:33.753
I can check and explain a shared relationship.

00:00:34.403 --> 00:00:37.603
A shared factor divides each number exactly.

00:00:37.823 --> 00:00:41.891
A shared multiple can be made as a whole-number multiple of each number.

00:00:42.111 --> 00:00:47.514
These are different questions, so decide whether you are looking for building blocks or for products.

00:00:47.734 --> 00:00:53.300
For example, two divides both twelve and eighteen exactly, so it is a shared factor.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:58.825
List the factors of eighteen: one, two, three, six, nine and eighteen.

00:00:59.045 --> 00:01:04.472
List the factors of thirty: one, two, three, five, six, ten, fifteen and thirty.

00:01:05.122 --> 00:01:08.937
The values in both lists are one, two, three and six.

00:01:09.157 --> 00:01:11.804
Each divides both numbers exactly.

00:01:12.024 --> 00:01:14.228
These are their shared factors.

00:01:14.878 --> 00:01:18.216
Now compare positive multiples of four and six.

00:01:18.436 --> 00:01:22.934
Four gives four, eight, twelve, sixteen, twenty and twenty-four.

00:01:23.154 --> 00:01:26.673
Six gives six, twelve, eighteen and twenty-four.

00:01:27.323 --> 00:01:30.494
Twelve and twenty-four appear in both lists.

00:01:30.714 --> 00:01:35.671
They are shared multiples because each is a whole-number multiple of both four and six.

00:01:36.321 --> 00:01:40.553
Eight divides twenty-four, but it does not divide thirty-six exactly.

00:01:40.773 --> 00:01:44.279
A factor of just one number is not a shared factor.

00:01:44.499 --> 00:01:47.326
Always check both numbers before choosing.

00:01:47.976 --> 00:01:53.025
Which of five, six, seven or eight is a factor of both twenty-four and thirty-six?

00:01:57.025 --> 00:01:58.465
Six works.

00:01:58.685 --> 00:02:03.160
Six times four is twenty-four and six times six is thirty-six.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:07.601
Eight divides twenty-four but not thirty-six, so it is not shared.

00:02:08.251 --> 00:02:13.717
Shared factors divide both numbers; shared multiples appear in both product patterns.

00:02:13.937 --> 00:02:18.101
Use complete lists or exact division to justify your decision.

00:02:18.321 --> 00:02:22.148
Explain the relationship rather than guessing from one example.

00:02:22.368 --> 00:02:25.775
Reflect on which model helped you and explain why.
